ds or now  tens of thousands of different rna  molecules  simultaneously within individual cells  in fixed  tissue samples and because of that we  can now do spatially resolved  single cell transfer comics and use  these measurements  to both discover and define cell types  and states while mapping out their  intricate spatial organization in intact  tissues  so today i'm going to tell you a little  bit about how this technology works how  it performs  i'll give you just a taste of the wide  variety of biological applications that  we are now tackling with this  and then i'll dive relatively deeply  into one i think very evocative  application  the use of this technique to discover  and map cell types in a portion of the  mouse brain  before i go too far though i want to  acknowledge that i'm a co-founder and  stakeholder  in a company that aims to commercialize  the murafish technology  that companies known as visgen though no  products or services of this gen were  used  to produce any of the data or results  that i'll present here today  so why would one want or need spatially  resolved  approaches to single cell  transcriptomics well i'm sure you're all  very well aware  of the revolution that we've seen in our  understanding of a wide variety of  tissues  driven by our ability to dissociate  capture and sequence individual cells  using technologies such as single cell  rna sequencing  so we can now take any complex tissue  dissociated  into individual cells extract from those  cells the rnas the express  and then quantify their expression at  the transcriptome scale using next  generation sequencing  that in turn has allowed us to now  discover and catalog cell types  in almost every organism and tissue  but in the process of dissociating these  cells and extracting these rnas we lose  important insights so we lose insight  into how the rnas are organized within  the cell  and that organization can be a very  potent form of post-transcriptional  regulation  that drives a wide variety of biological  phenomena in addition we lose insight  into how these cells are  actually organized within the tissue and  as we know  tissue organization plays an intricate  role in how  individual cell types communicate with  one another and how the behavior of the  tissue as a whole emerges from the  behavior of the individual cell types  and states that comprise it  and so what we wanted to do was to  develop technology that had the  strengths of single cell rna sequencing  but could preserve this spatial context  and that's what we've done with merfish  so murfish is based upon a very powerful  technology known as single molecule  fluorescence institute hybridization or  fish  in this approach you fix your sample  permeabilize the membrane of the cells  and then hybridize to it a set of  fluorescently labeled dna oleg and  whose sequences are complementary to  different regions of the rna of interest  this concentrates a large number of  fluorophores in each and every molecule  of that rna  such that when you image this sample  with an epi fluorescence microscope  you now see bright fluorescent puncture  for each molecule this allows you to  count the number of molecules per cell  and of course see their organization  within the cell and  the organization that sell within the  context of the sample as a whole  so our goal with murfish was to take  this powerful technique and multiplex it  we do so by assigning to each rna a  unique binary barcode and then we read  out those barcodes in the following way  we state our sample using fairly  conventional single molecule fish  approaches  but with one important distinction  instead of targeting a single rna we  talk at every rna that has a one in the  first bit of the barcode  we get an image like this we'll remove  this signal now restain the sample  targeting every rna that has a one in  the second bit of this bar code  we'll get a different pattern of  fluorescence which we will then image  and extinguish  and we'll repeat this process for each  bit in these barcodes  such that the fluorescence on off  pattern we see across the stack images  defines the barcode and thus the rna  now one of the important features of  this is that we also  leverage bar coding schemes that allow  us to detect  and correct when individual errors can  happen so not only can we  identify large number of rnas via the  large number of barcodes produced by  this method  but we can do so with very high fidelity  as i'll show you here in a slide  so here's an example of an actual  murfish measurement what you see here on  the left  is an image of a human osteosarcoma cell  culture  after the first round of murphy's  imaging each bright fluorescent spot is  a single rna molecule  we can zoom in on one small portion of  one of the three cells you see in this  image  and you can see that one molecule is  present which gives us a one in the  first bit  we remove this signal resame the sample  other molecules are now fluorescent but  this molecule is no longer fluorescent  giving us a zero on the second bit  we do this again and the third bit this  molecule is now fluorescent again and i  think at this point you get the idea  in this case we'll do this 13 more times  to build up a stack of 16 images  this molecule is on the first the third  the eighth and the thirteenth image  giving us the barcode that you see here  we have this barcode or similar barcodes  i should say  for all of the 140 different types of  rnas stained in the sample  allowing us to reconstruct an image like  this where each marker is in rna  and the color is its identity and so you  can see that these  allow this approach allows us to  optically barcode and identify  individual rna molecules  and we've died we've extensively  benchmarked this technique so we know  that it now has a large dynamic range  near 100 molecular detection efficiency  and faithful detection even of a single  molecular copy  per cell and by increasing or changing  the length of these barcodes we can  tune the multiplexing the demands of our  specific question  we can identify hundreds of thousands or  now 10 000 different mrnas  simultaneously within individual cells  using merfish  because this is an imaging technology  and imaging can be quite rapid  we can actually perform these  measurements in tens if not 100 000  cells  in each measurement so this can be a  very high throughput technique as well  so with this ability to do massively  multiplexed single molecule imaging  within individual cells  we can now ask a pretty broad range of  questions for example  we can use the natural variation in gene  expression from cell to cell  to identify sets of genes that co-vary  and thus are likely co-regulated  so we can identify or delineate  regulatory modules  we of course know exactly where each and  every one of those copies was found  within the cells  we can map the internal organization  transcriptome and we can do this in a  wide variety of  cell types states we can use the fact  that gene expression is a faithful  measure of the state of the cell to  infer  properties of that cell not just type  but state  we can also use this technology to  measure synthetic rnas not just native  rnas and these synthetic rnas can serve  as bar codes that allow us to identify  individual cells for example  cells that carry different genetic  perturbations and so this opens up a  wide variety of exciting biotechnology  applications as well  and then finally we can use the gene  expression measurements we have for  individual cells  to define their type and their state and  in many cases discover  unanticipated types and states all while  mapping their intricate organization and  tissue  and it's this application i'm going to  primarily focus on for the remainder of  my talk  so i'm going to tell you about a  published application of murfish for  discovering and mapping cell types and  states this application  was from the mouse brain in particular a  portion known as the pre-optic region of  the hypothalamus this was work done in  collaboration  with catherine dillock while i was still  a postdoc in chile sean's laboratory  now what you see here on the left is a  coronal and a satchel slice in the mouth  frame and the pre-optic region is the  region in the black box  and approximately in the region the  dashed line  now this is an interesting brain region  because it's responsible in part  for the control of a wide variety of  homeostatic functions so it has circuits  that control how  a mouse responds to hunger thirst or  fever  and it also controls higher level  functions that are nonetheless  non-learned social functions for example  how a mouse responds to an aggressive  perturbation or a situation that  involves mating  or parenting we know through the work of  a variety of labs that there are neurons  in this region of the brain  that comprise circuits that are  activated and in part control these  behaviors  but what wasn't clear was the diversity  of neuronal types that were found within  this region  and so we wanted to use murphish to ask  the simple question what are the  different types of neurons in this  portion of brain  and how are they linked to these  hypothalamic functions  but not just what neurons are there but  how are they spatially organized how do  they distribute  into the already defined sub-regions in  the hypothalamus that have been linked  to a variety of different hypothalamic  functions  and then can we go beyond just  identifying these neurons  based on their molecular profiles and  also mapping them spatially  to inferring their functional  organization into circuits  now morpheus is well suited for all of  these measurements but because this was  the first large-scale test of this  technology for discovery and mapping  cell types  we wanted to have an independent measure  of the cell type diversity that was  present there  so the first thing we did was dissect  out dissociate single cells  from the pre-optic region of multiple  male and female mice capture them  using the 10x chromium platform and  perform single-cell rna sequencing that  produced the data that you see here on  the left  this is a matrix in which individual  rows represent different genes  columns or different cells and colors  expression those genes in those cells  we can then cluster these cells using  standard statistical methods  and identify those clusters based on the  genes they express  and as expected we find all of the major  classes of cells so i'm going to  anticipate  in this portion of the brain we can then  visualize this high dimensional data in  two dimensions  using a technology known as t-sne where  each cell here in this 2d space for each  marker here in this 2d space is a cell  and it falls next to other cells  expressed the same genes the colors are  now indicated by the  clustering of these cells and we can  then take out our inhibitory and  excitatory neurons  marked here in red and yellow subject  into a second round of clustering and we  found  about 66 different types of neurons in  this portion of the brain  we then in parallel performed murfish on  exactly the same brain region taken from  different mice  we measured in total 155 different genes  with this technique  taken from three different categories  first category were classically defined  markers of major cell types so  genes that define inhibitory neurons  excitatory neurons oligodendrocytes  and so forth the second class of genes  were inspired by what we discovered with  our single cell rna sequencing and were  included  because they could discriminate  different neuronal types  but we didn't want to just define  neuronal types we also wanted to learn  something about their biology  and it's known from a wide variety of  prior work that neuropeptide and hormone  signaling is an important mode of  communication in the pre-optic region  and so we included in our panel a third  category of genes which was a  comprehensive set of neuropeptides and  neuropeptide and hormone receptors  and the receptors turn out to be an  important group because these are genes  that can be quite functionally relevant  even when expressed at very low levels  and despite its many strengths one of  the weaknesses of single cell rna  sequencing  is that it captures a relatively modest  fraction of what's actually expressed in  the cell  so it can be challenging to extract  statistically robust measurements  of these slowly expressed but  functioning relevant genes by contrast  murphy has single molecule sensitivity  so we hope we could do a better job here  what you see here on the left is a  result from a murfish measurement  we took a two millimeter by two  millimeter by six hundred micron block  after the pre-optic region  sectioned it into ten micron thick  sections sampled every  fifth section i read those on cover  slips and measure them with murfish  what you see here on the left is a 200  by 200 micron field of view from one  such slice  each white dot is again a single rna  molecule we would include with these  measurements  co-stains that will allow us to  automatically define the boundaries of  these cells that's what's marked in  white  and then we can color these rnas based  on their identity as determined by  more fish and i think you can  immediately see from this  image the transcriptional diversity that  is present  within just a small portion of this  tissue you have cells that are  expressing predominantly red rnas  green rnas blue rnas and so forth these  are  a visual representation of the molecular  fingerprints that define  different cell types now to give you a  sense of scale over which we can perform  these measurements  let's zoom back to a single two by two  millimeter slice that's what you see  here the white box is the field of view  that i just showed you  and now we don't have a color palette  that can accurately describe to ri 155  different genes  so i'm going to simplify this by just  showing you eight so we can walk through  the spatial distribution of just eight  different genes  collected in this single slice and i  think you can immediately see  the richness in gene expression across  these large tissue areas  and by leveraging the high throughput of  murfish we could in total  characterize gene expression in 1.1  million cells  with tissue collected from 31 different  animals and a total integrated tissue  area  of over 700 millimeters squared  because we have the boundaries of these  cells and the rnas that fall within  those boundaries we have an expression  profile for each and every one of these  cells  we can now analyze these using exactly  the same statistical toolbox developed  for single cell rna sequencing  so unsupervised clustering and tc  visualization that's what you see here  and what we found was that 155 genes  was more than sufficient to find all the  major classes of cells we expect to find  in this brain  as you can see here and now we could  actually ask a very interesting question  we've now characterized gene expression  in this region of the brain  using a technology that dissociates and  sequences cells  and one that images rnas directly within  intact tissue  both technologies find the same classes  as cells but to what degree do their  expression profiles agree  so we performed a pairwise pearson  correlation analysis  in this matrix each row represents  a cell type identified with murfish each  column  the comparable type identified with  sequencing and the strong diagonal here  indicates that  these two technologies agree very nicely  the fact these correlation coefficients  on this diagonal are all very close to  one  means that this agreement is actually  quantitative so it's a nice  cross-validation of both techniques  so murfish with 155 genes can define  these cell types  the advantage of course is that we never  dissociated the cell so we get an atlas  of this tissue  essentially for free that's what you see  here each black box is a two by two  millimeter slice  there are twelve slices taken from a  single animal and they are arrayed from  the anterior to  posterior portion of the brain left to  right the gray boundaries  mark individual cells you can help color  these cells based on the identities  determined by the expression profiles  as measured by merfish so here are our  inhibitory neurons  our excitatory neurons mature  myelinating oligodendrocytes  oligodendrocyte precursors microglia  astrocytes  endothelial cells neural cells  and ependymal cells these are ciliated  cells that drive the movement of  cervical spinal fluid  and you see that they form a single cell  layer flanking a structure known as the  third ventricle  filled with that fluid and so we can now  put back  all of these cell types to reveal the  intricate organization  of this portion of the brain from a  single mouse  now we can take our inhibitory  excitatory neurons as measured by  murfish  subjecting to a second round of  unsupervised clustering in this case we  find 70 different types of neurons  we can then do the same type of pearson  correlation analysis  with single cell sequencing that i  presented a few slides ago we see a nice  strong diagonal again  providing a nice validation in these two  technologies but we can go one step  further  and look to find a correspondence  between the neural types we find in  murfish  and neuronal types we find in sequencing  and we did the simplest thing one could  imagine  which is to pull out the largest and  statistically distinguishable  pearson correlation coefficients those  are the elements marked here in this  matrix  now what's nice about this is that we  find that the vast majority of the  neuronal types that we see in murfish  have a corresponding type in sequencing  that is a nice cross validation again  but one thing i want you to notice  is that this correspondence is not  always one to one there are instances  in which murfish is able to subdivide a  single neuronal type  that sequencing identified and what we  find in most of those instances  is that these subdivisions are defined  by the expression of  lowly expressed but functionally  relevant  receptors and so the sensitivity of  murfish allows us  to discover cell types that we were  incapable of discovering  with the statistical approaches that we  used with single cell sequencing  we can also take a look at the spatial  distribution of these neurons here again  we see our 12 slices per animal  array left to right gray are all cells  and red green and blue  mark three of the more abundant  inhibitory neuronal types i want you to  see that these patterns are bilaterally  symmetric  and that they change in striking ways  over relatively short distances  along the anterior posterior axis these  patterns are also nicely reproduced  across replicated animals  and i don't want to go here into the  details  of what these spatial distributions mean  there's a lot that they tell us about  the organization  and the principles of organization  hypothalamus but what i want you to take  from this  is that if we look at nine different  types of the 70 types of neurons that we  found  they all have their own unique spatial  distributions so there's a remarkable  spatial richness  in this small portion of the brain  now i want to tell you about one last  experiment we did in this work  what we did is we took a female mouse we  added a pup to its cage  it turns out that that mouse will  actually go and retrieve the pup bring  it back to its nest and care  for it even if it's not its own pup  that's called the non-learned or  instinctive parental behavior  after that behavior we then sacrificed  the mess and performed murphys in its  brain  and included one extra gene in our panel  this was the gene c-phos which is  rapidly expressed in neurons  after activity we clustered and found  all of our semi-type of neurons and then  looked to see which of these neurons  were expressing c phos what you see here  is a bar for each of our neurons  and the height of that bar indicates the  enrichment in cfos  expression what we find is in these  virgin female mice that have just  expressed this parenting behavior  there's one and only one type of neuron  with a statistically significant  enrichment in cfos  we can now do exactly the same  experiment but in male mice  and the results are strikingly different  those mice will often kill those pups  what we find is they have a different  set of neurons that are now activated  now we can do a very interesting  experiment we can take a male mouse  three weeks after it's sexually  experienced  it will now care for those pups exactly  like female mice will  now what we see is that this parental  neuron is activated  in the background of an activation  pattern reminiscent of the aggression  and so i think you can get the point  here we can now stimulate these mice  with a variety of  known stimuli that trigger hypothalamic  functions and now systematically go  through and functionally annotate the  neurons that we've discovered  and one point i want to make here is  that the neurons we find that  discriminate aggression and parenting  were not separately clustered in our  sequencing data these are defined  by a handful of lowly expressed  receptors and we only see these neural  types  in our more sensitive or fish  measurements  and so that i want to just leave you  with a sense of murfish murphish is a  massively multiplexed single molecule  imaging technique that  functions by taking a series of images  all of the same sample in which  different subsets of rnas are less  simply labeled in each image  such that across the stack of image each  rna has a unique fluorescence on a  pattern that serves as a barcode that  identifies it  we can bootstrap this technology to  the multiplexing of hundreds thousands  10  000 different mrnas so near  transcriptome scale measurements  we can do so with very high performance  near 100 detection efficiency  very low detection limits and large  dynamic ranges and we can quantify tens  of millions of rna copies in tens of  thousands of cells per day at a cost of  less than a fraction of a penny per cell  and we can use this technology to now  create molecularly defined atlases of  complex tissues  for example in the hypothalamus we  created such an atlas in the preoptic  region  we used this atlas to discover really a  striking  diversity of neuronal types in such a  small portion of the brain  and to map them out and to functionally  annotate them  and the spatial organization that we  observed has very interesting  implications for both the circuits that  are present in this region of the brain  as well as a developmental process that  gives rise to them  the last point i want to leave you with  is the fact that the technologies that  we developed and used in the brain  are not specific to the brain and so  that means that murfish can be used to  create similar molecular defined atlases  in a wide range of tissues which is  exactly what my laboratory  is now doing so with that i want to  thank say thank you to all the people  who are involved in this work  mainly the work that i presented here  for all of the work that i presented  here today  was directed my postdoctoral time in  shawway's  schwalms laboratory and the tissue work  was all a close collaboration with  catherine delox laboratory  we had a really great group of people  working on these projects uh stephen  eichorn dj bombamoku and eric von were  involved in the tissue work  and then how aleister stephen junjie  ping tien hazen and george  were all heavily involved in different  aspects of murphy's development  and we had some excellent help in  clustering from karthik  shakhar and navid regislam i also want  to thank  members of my new laboratory who have  been helping me get this technology  established  at boston children's and harvard medical  school and are now using this to tackle  a wide range of questions  and i look forward to telling you about  those results sometime in the future  so thank you very much for your time and  attention  all right so now we're going to open  this up  to questions  again if you have any questions that you  haven't asked  there should be a option for you to  add those questions to the list in the  lower left  of your uh window there  and so sandra will be joining us here i  think in a moment to answer a few  questions but  uh in the meanwhile  uh i will go ahead and start with a  murphish question  so we have a question from the audience  that reads does murphy require  fresh brain and can it work with frozen  human brain  so the answer is that a murphy is  actually a very general  technology it can work with a wide  variety of different  um preparation methods we have worked  with  freshly frozen brain as well  as um heavily fixed brain  but it does require fixed tissues  because you are repetitively imaging the  sample so you need to have  molecules that are anchored in place  great uh so sandra there are a few  questions here for you you hear me jeff  yes i can hear you now great and so  um i'll just start at the bottom  do you perform a strip and reprobe of  tissues so you can obtain  all the cell types in one single site  and then there's a nice compliment to  say that the images are impressive which  i completely agree  thanks jeff um so so the method um  it's fairly straightforward we we  incubate with antibodies  the standard way is three antibodies and  then after imaging we we photobleach so  the antibodies remain  uh attached to the tissue and then we  reapply an additional  three antibodies and continue with  cycles of application  and then photobleaching so there's no uh  stripping per se although some methods  um that are similar to this one use  stripping  we find that t