ery  but we use a machine learning based  bio image analysis tool called elastic  which is really great  we generate segmentation masks then do  single cell calls of  fluorescence intensity and then it goes  on i mean it just goes on and on and on  from there in terms of cell type calling  um neighborhood analyses there's really  a there's really a lot to it and we can  go on indefinitely but i  i invite you to look at our at our  github the santa gada lab github the  lab of systems pharmacology github with  peter sorger and also a few of our  papers  uh in e-life nature protocols and nature  cell biology which will get you  a good view of how this works but it's  it really i don't i jeff i mean  this for us this is like our main thing  i i think  the the key thing is the underestimation  uh  in terms of the amount of computational  time and effort that's required  the acquisition is really rather  straightforward in comparison  yeah i would completely agree that what  is exciting about these  extremely multiplexed images uh is the  amount information you get  from them but you have to you have to  computationally extract that  it's a non trivial problem it's not  solved  and uh i think it is a place that needs  a lot of active research  a place where innovations can make a  huge difference absolutely  jeff should i ask you a question from  the list list so  so there's one that says is there any  limit for low abundancy transcript  transcription uh transcripts  sorry yeah so in murfish they're they're  um they're in practice is no limit we  have  in every measurement we include a series  of bar codes that could identify rnas  yet we don't so those serve as an  internal control that measures our false  positive rate and  that will vary slightly from measurement  to measurement but it's consistently at  a level of  uh less than a fraction of a transcript  per cell  so that means that if you count a single  copy of a transcript you can actually  trust that measurement 